#
# `navigator.maxTouchPoints` reported **10** on a persona claiming an Apple M3, and it was the
# loudest contradiction assertion 6 still scored on the shipping engine -- one of two remaining
# floors, the other being WebGPU, which needs a host this one cannot be. Apple has never shipped a
# touchscreen Mac, so the count alone refutes the platform, and `(any-pointer: coarse)` matched
# beside it, which refutes it a second time in one more line of CSS.
#
# ONE HOOK, FIVE SURFACES. `SlowWebPreferenceCache::Update()` fills six `WebPreferences` fields from
# `ui::MaxTouchPoints()` and `ui::GetAvailablePointerAndHoverTypes()`; `Load()` copies them into
# every renderer's prefs and is called from exactly one place
# (content/browser/web_contents/web_contents_impl.cc:3800). Five web surfaces read the result:
# `navigator.maxTouchPoints` (via `Settings::GetMaxTouchPoints`), `(pointer:)`, `(any-pointer:)`,
# `(hover:)` and `(any-hover:)` (via `MediaValues::CalculateAvailablePointerTypes` and its three
# siblings). Overriding `NavigatorEvents::maxTouchPoints` -- which is where the counterpart does it --
# moves one of the five and leaves the other four contradicting it. This patch overrides `Load()`, in
# the browser process, so all five come from one number.
#
# THE NUMBER. `--fingerprint-max-touch-points=<n>` first, then the claimed platform, then the host
# untouched. The switch is honoured with or without a seed and on any claim, which is
# `--fingerprint-hardware-concurrency`'s shape: a value the user typed is not a cross-platform
# substitution, so it is not gated on one. The platform rung is gated on
# `ShouldPresentOtherPlatform()`, so a seedless launch and a Windows-claim-on-Windows-host both fall
# through -- `906`'s same-OS argument, third application.
#
# macOS answers **0 structurally**, and that is the strongest kind of claim this patch set makes:
# ui/base/BUILD.gn:433-446 builds `pointer_device_win.cc` for Windows, `_android` / `_ios` / `_linux`
# / `_web_engine` for theirs, and `pointer/pointer_device_default.cc` for everything else -- macOS
# included. That file is 25 lines of constants (`MaxTouchPoints()` returns 0,
# `GetAvailablePointerAndHoverTypesImpl()` returns `{POINTER_TYPE_FINE, HOVER_TYPE_HOVER}`,
# `GetTouchScreensAvailability()` returns `NONE`). No Mac reports a touchscreen through this API even
# if Apple ships one. Linux answers **0 modally** -- `pointer_device_linux.cc:33-40` reads
# `DeviceDataManager`, so the platform does not decide, and 0 is chosen because the alternative is
# this Windows host's digitizer count, which is a leak rather than a guess. The header says which row
# is which out loud.
#
# The four pointer / hover fields are derived from the count by Chromium's own rules, with a line
# citation each at the call site: a touchscreen *adds* COARSE beside FINE rather than replacing it
# (`pointer_device_{win,linux}.cc`), and FINE and HOVER win their primary slots whenever available
# (`pointer_device.cc:47-66`), so `primary_pointer_type` and `primary_hover_type` are computed, never
# chosen.
#
# THREE THINGS THIS DOES NOT DO, and all three are re-scopings the control measurement produced
# rather than choices. Stock Chrome 151, the packaged engine and this build were read back to back in
# one minute (Veil/docs/MIGRATION-UNGOOGLED.md 6.18):
#
# - **`getBattery()` is left alone.** The inventory's row reads "`charging: true, level: 1` is the
#   desktop default and is fine; a laptop persona that never discharges is not". Measured: all four
#   fields are already the canonical AC-powered set -- `charging: true`, `chargingTime: 0`,
#   `dischargingTime: Infinity`, `level: 1` -- and byte-identical on stock Chrome, so there is no
#   divergence to close. They are also what a real MacBook reports plugged in at full charge, and
#   `011`'s renderer string names a *part* (`Apple M3`), not a chassis, so nothing in the persona
#   claims a machine that must be discharging. clearcote's `150` pins these four to exactly the
#   values they already hold, which on this host is a no-op with a comment. Fabricating a discharge
#   would mean a value that changes over time and must agree across three realms, which is a new
#   surface rather than a closed contradiction.
# - **The generic sensors are left alone, and the reason is in this tree.**
#   `services/device/generic_sensor/platform_sensor_provider_mac.cc:17-25` is
#   `CreateSensorInternal` running `std::move(callback).Run(nullptr)` for **every** sensor type, with
#   a comment saying the hardware pokes stopped working long ago. So a real Mac fails every
#   `Sensor.start()`, and this host fails it identically: `Accelerometer`, `Gyroscope`,
#   `LinearAccelerationSensor`, `GravitySensor` and both orientation sensors are present with
#   permission `granted` and answer `NotReadableError: Could not connect to a sensor`, while
#   `Magnetometer` and `AmbientLightSensor` are absent on every platform because their constructors
#   are behind build-level `RuntimeEnabledFeatures`. T02 refuted this task's original acceptance
#   ("sensors absent on a desktop claim") and this refutes the replacement: the set already *is* the
#   claimed platform's. One residual is named rather than patched -- a Windows host with a working
#   accelerometer would stream readings where a Mac cannot, and suppressing that is unmeasurable on
#   this host, so it is filed with T08's borrowed-hardware items instead of guessed at.
# - **Gamepad is left alone.** `navigator.getGamepads()` returns 4 slots with nothing connected on
#   all three binaries, and the array length is a Blink constant rather than a platform fact. Nothing
#   measurable to move.
#
# NO `--disable-spoofing` KEY, and none is added. Veil has no field that could emit one for this
# surface, and a switch value nothing can send is the same lie as a switch no Chromium has
# (CLAUDE.md, "Flag dialect"). Leaving `--fingerprint-max-touch-points` unset is the opt-out: the
# platform rung then answers only for a cross-platform claim, which is the case where the host's
# count is a leak.
#
# The switch is declared in `components/ungoogled/ungoogled_switches` and read in the browser
# process **only**. It is deliberately absent from
# `RenderProcessHostImpl::PropagateBrowserCommandLineToRenderer`'s allow-list: no renderer code reads
# it, and propagating a switch into a process that never reads it would be a fifth instance of the
# violation `000` already carries four of -- declared, propagated, read by nothing (6.9).
#
# Veil-authored `9NN` numbering: `961` has no `061` to correct, so it is in the `950`-`999` range
# reserved for patches with no direct counterpart.

